        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Astronaut crews for long-duration multi-national missions will endure many physical challenges and psychological stressors, some largely predictable in type and timing and others unpredictable. Crews are likely to be diverse with respect to educational background, skill set, ethnicity, gender, leadership/followership styles etc., yet they must form a cohesive team, and continue to function together at a high level of objective performance and remain responsive to mission support over the duration of the mission. Crew cohesion will be more fragile at times of high stress and fatigue, yet those are the times when performance must be unimpaired if the crew is to succeed. Adding to the challenge, the pool from which crews must be selected may be significantly constrained by other factors, such as past radiation exposure.
<div>
In order to enable and advance long-duration human space exploration, the investigators proposed to study individual and crew characteristics that affected crew function and performance, by measuring both characteristics and performance on a range of simulated missions in analog environments. Based on the correlations found, they developed a predictive model of the relationship between crew composition and performance. They validated and enhanced this model via data collected on four 4-month Hawaii Space Exploration Analog and Simulation (HI-SEAS) missions, and use the results to provide NASA with a set of tools to optimize its crew composition strategies.
</div><div><br></div><div>
APPROACH:<br>
Crews consisting of six individuals were selected based on balancing gender and primary skill sets, cognitive function, personality, and technical skills. An outcome of this selection matching was to reduce extraneous between-group variability and consequently allowed us to expect larger effect sizes for critical measures.
</div><div>
Prior to each analog, the crew had a two-week familiarization training session at HI-SEAS, during which period the crew learned about the habitat systems and procedures (including emergency procedures) and undertook all the pre-mission assessments.
</div><div>
During the analog, the crew was tested on team processes factors, team effectiveness and on the different characteristics that contribute to team effectiveness.  These criteria were used to develop and validate a predictive model.  For assessing team processes and team effectiveness, there were two planned data collection components.  The Experience Sampling Methodology (ESM) protocol has been used in other Antarctic and NASA analogs to benchmark team cohesion, collaboration, and effective functioning.  Crewmembers filled out daily surveys and assessments to assess events, affects and reactions to the day.  This primary ESM data collection was supplemented with pre-mission assessment of individual differences (i.e., background, personality) and post-mission reflections. The ESM data allowed dynamic analyses that can track and establish baselines on the nature of team functioning for the duration of the mission. These dynamic team process and outcome data also provided key criteria for identifying which personal characteristics are most strongly associated with effective team functioning.  The second data collection utilized a commercial sociometric interaction badge (SS), which post-processes data, and a prototype wireless monitoring “badge”, which streams data real time, that investigators had been developing to assess team interactions in situ.  Both monitored the subject’s intensity of physical movement, vocal activity and intensity, heart rate, and face-time (distance between persons facing one another). Team interaction analytics could be performed either in real-time or post-processing.
During the study, the crew was tested on team composition as it relates to social personality (cooperation, punishment, productivity, and fairness motivation). For measuring team composition, there were two planned software components.  The Cooperation and Objective Human Econometric Social Interactions for Organizations and Networks (COHESION) software was in development, but was based on the investigators’ prototype, Team Performance Task (TPT) software, which is being used in HI-SEAS as well as in NASA’s Human Exploration Research Analog (HERA).  Crewmembers completed three test sessions during the pre-mission week, during the mission, and post mission. Each test session consisted of a saliva sample and brief cohesion questionnaire followed by three 3-minute COHESION trials. Psychophysiological variables (heart rate, temperature, skin conductance) were measured continuously in real-time throughout each session.  Saliva samples were subsequently analyzed for behaviorally active hormones and stress biomarkers (cortisol, alpha-amylase, testosterone, progesterone, and estradiol).
</div><div>
Communication analyses were performed on the crewmembers. This testing protocol was previously used in other analogs (bed rest, HERA, and HI-SEAS) as well as the use of historical data (i.e. Apollo mission crew-ground communications) that resulted in theoretically grounded linguistic detectors that can use text narratives and conversational transcripts to extract measures such as power dynamics, crew social comfort, affect, mental wellbeing and mood, attitudes towards specific topics, temporal focus, and self-versus others focus. In this effort, investigators planned to extend the techniques to evaluate individual and team characteristics relevant to crew composition.  Investigators utilized data collected from the Mars Desert Research Station (MDRS) to identify correlations between detected psycho-social dimensions and crew function to assess communication analysis. The result was relevant measures that can be integrated into a model for crew composition, which can then be adapted and applied for the HI-SEAS crew selection.
</div><div>
HI-SEAS offers the opportunity to develop effective and novel strategies to enhance Mission Support (MS) and crew operational interaction under different levels of crew autonomy relevant to distant space exploration and remote isolated environments. Crewmembers experienced low and partial autonomy for four months. Daily reports of the implementation of the weekly operational plan were a metric for assessing the consequence of operational planning and management directed by MS.
</div><div>
RESULTS:<br><div>The results showed that the battery of selection metrics led
to a successful M5, characterized by little internal crew conflict (task or
relationship) and relatively high levels of cohesion throughout the entire 8
months. <o:p></o:p></div>

<div>M6 was seen to have scored at least a standard deviation
away from M5 and generally in the more problematic direction, it is possible
that the metrics might be of value for composing future long-duration mission
crews. It is possible that such norms should be differentiated for teams
operating in ICE conditions as compared teams in other industries/environments
(similar to the teamwork skills measure) as slight variations in scores on
these metrics might have a major influence on performance and cohesion in such
conditions. While our study provides some initial thoughts on what those norms
might be, additional research in other ICE environments is needed before making
definitive cutoff scores for any measure included in our battery.<o:p></o:p></div>

<div>With regard to cohesion and conflict, as noted above, the M5
crew had more conflict with aspects of mission control than with themselves.
Given that this conflict began rather early in the mission, it may have served
as a rallying point for the crew to help ignite their social cohesion. Overall,
task and social cohesion were both rated highly in the first four months
in-mission, with task cohesion higher than social cohesion, and both decreasing
significantly over time (Omnibus fixed effects Fs > 8.0, ps < .009).<o:p></o:p></div>

<div>Although time in mission was a strong influence on behavior,
Likelihood Ratio Tests indicate significant model improvement by inclusion of
the individual and team random coefficients (ps < 0.001), with intraclass
correlations revealing considerable variance in selfishness, cooperation, and
productivity behaviors accounted for by individual crew member (ICCs = 0.421,
0.298, 0.195) and the specific teams they were a part of (0.005, 0.223, 0.249).
Together, these data suggest that gender itself or gender ratio of a team are
not significant contributors to small team performance in isolation and
confinement.<o:p></o:p></div></div><div></div>
        </div>

